sentences.l:
“Ah, truly a great wonder this with my eyes I behold,
how again, once more, has stood up, having escaped the death-fates,
Hector;

But some one of the gods again rescued and saved
Hector, who indeed of many Danaans the knees loosed,
thus also now to be, I suspect;

but we ourselves, as many as claim to be best in the army,
let us take our stand, if first we may hold him back, meeting him,
holding up our spears;

And the Argives stood their ground in a close mass, and arose a battle-cry
shrill from both sides, and from the bowstring the arrows
leapt;

Hector, for his part, Stichius and Arcesilaus killed,
the one, of the bronze-shirted Boeotians a leader,
the other, of great-hearted Menestheus a trusty companion;

but he dwelt
in Phylace, away from his fatherland, having killed a man,
a kinsman of his stepmother Eriopis, whom Oileus had;

and they, with him,
all, having shouted, held the chariot-drawing horses
with a wondrous din;

So these by the ships were held back, remaining,
and to one another calling and to all the gods,
hands lifting up, loudly prayed, each man;
